==============

Branching Strategy

===============

We follow a feature-based branching strategy to ensure the main codebase remains stable and production-ready at all times.

Developers create short-lived feature branches from the development branch and work independently on new features, enhancements, or bug fixes. During development, they perform coding and unit testing to verify that the functionality works as expected.

Once the development work is completed, a Pull Request (PR) is raised for code review. After the review and approval process, the code is merged into the develop branch.

The CI/CD pipeline then builds the application and deploys it to the QA environment, where functional and regression testing are performed. If all tests pass successfully, the same build artifact is promoted to the UAT/Stage environment for final validation.

After receiving business approval in UAT, the tested build is deployed to the Production environment. The main branch is considered a long-lived branch containing only stable and production-ready code, while feature branches are short-lived and removed after they are merged.

==================

DevOps life cycle

=================

In our DevOps process, developers first write code and perform unit testing in the Development (DEV) environment.

After successful development and initial verification, the application is deployed to the Quality Assurance (QA) environment. In QA, testers perform functional testing to validate business requirements and regression testing to ensure that newly added changes have not impacted existing functionality.

Once QA testing is successfully completed, the build is promoted to the Stage/UAT environment. This environment closely resembles production and is used for performance testing, load testing, and user acceptance testing.

After all validations and approvals are completed, the same build artifact is released to the Production environment, where end users and customers access the application.

By following this promotion process, we ensure that only thoroughly tested and validated code reaches production.

================

MLOps Lifecycle

================

In MLOps, the process starts with collecting and preparing data from various sources.

The collected data is used to train a machine learning model. Once training is completed, the model is validated to ensure it meets the required accuracy and performance standards.

Before deployment, several testing activities are performed, including Unit Testing, Pipeline Validation, Model Validation, Integration Testing, End-to-End Inference Testing, and Shadow Testing. These tests verify that the model functions correctly and integrates properly with the surrounding systems.

After successful validation, the model is deployed to production and continuously monitored for performance, accuracy, and data drift. If necessary, the model is retrained using updated data to maintain prediction quality.

===============

Code Promotion

===============

Code promotion refers to moving the same build artifact from one environment to another after successful testing and approval.

Developers create feature branches and perform coding and unit testing in the DEV environment. Once development is completed, the code undergoes peer review and approval before being merged into the main or develop branch.

A CI/CD pipeline then generates a build artifact, such as a JAR, WAR. Following the "Build Once, Deploy Many" principle, the same artifact is promoted through all environments without rebuilding.

The build is first deployed to QA, where functional and regression testing are conducted. After QA approval, the build is promoted to the Stage/UAT environment for performance testing and user acceptance testing.

Once all validations are completed and stakeholders approve the release, the exact same build is deployed to Production.

This promotion strategy ensures consistency across environments and guarantees that the code tested in lower environments is identical to the code deployed to production.
